{"slug": "orifice-plate-sizing-guide", "targets": ["plate diameter"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">Orifice Plate Diameter Table by Beta Ratio</h2><p><strong>Plate diameter</strong> in orifice sizing usually means the bore diameter d, the hole that creates the differential pressure. It is found from the beta ratio, β = d ÷ D, where D is the internal pipe diameter. Once the flow calculation gives β, the bore is simply d = β × D.</p><p>The table shows bore diameters for two pipe internal diameters across the beta range allowed by ISO and ASME, 0.10 to 0.75.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Beta ratio β</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Bore d for D = 100 mm</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Bore d for D = 200 mm</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Comment</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.10</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">20 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Below the 12.5 mm minimum for D = 100 mm</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.20</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">20 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">40 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">API minimum beta</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.40</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">40 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">80 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Start of the ideal range</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.50</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">50 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Ideal range</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.60</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">60 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">120 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">End of the ideal range</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.70</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">70 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">140 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">API upper limit for gas and steam</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.75</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">75 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">150 mm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Upper limit for liquids</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Checks after finding the bore</h3><ol><li>Confirm the bore is at least 12.5 mm (ISO, ASME) or 12.7 mm (API).</li><li>Confirm β is inside the allowed range for the fluid.</li><li>Correct D and d to operating temperature if they were measured at another temperature.</li><li>Specify the sharp upstream edge, with edge radius under 0.0004d and a 90° ± 0.3° corner.</li></ol><p>A β between 0.4 and 0.6 gives a good balance between measurable differential pressure and permanent pressure loss.</p>"}